s have been  distorted and encourage them to take too  much risk distorted by a government  backstop distorted by a so-called  trader's option that individuals within  a big bank may want to take more risk  because they'll get a big bonus  and the kind of financial vehicle that  we have that has fewer  of these distortive incentives are hedge  funds they have the managers have their  own money in the fund so they have an  incentive to avoid too much risk they  are not backstopped by the government so  they don't have that safety net they are  monitored very aggressively by their  creditors  so that means another safety check so  although they're not perfect and they do  fail sometimes they fail safely and it's  a better place for risk to be managed  than all the alternatives that i can  think of  it is of course a slightly hard sell  though because of you know i mean all  when ordinary people who are seeing  their incomes cut losing their jobs look  at these extraordinary characters you  know this fascinating story of how the  industry developed  looked all the money involved and  looked at all the freewheeling it's very  hard for them to not think well hang on  a second and why are they off in  um the cayman islands you know why are  they doing all this clever mathematical  stuff we don't understand i mean that's  right very easy targets and the answer  is more progressive taxation tax the  profits by all means but don't shut down  this form of investment management  because it's better and healthier for  financial for the financial system  and for the economy writ large and if  they were to tax the profits i mean how  much money do you think they could raise  build a few schools in the midwest or  well i mean a bit more healthcare i've  never been asked that question but i  mean if we just did back of the envelope  the top 25 um hedge fund managers on the  list of the you know the biggest earners  you know made about 25 billion dollars  between them  okay what just last when just in one  year yesterday in 2009 i think 2009  which was supposed to be a bad year for  most of the financial industries they  made 25  billion so if we just hiked their taxes  by 20 percentage points  right you make 5 billion right there i  mean 5 billion you know that's a few  schools  wow  well that's certainly a both inspiring  but also quite encouraging lesson to end  with so thank you very much indeed  sebastian for  providing a truly fascinating look at  the industry which is both very  interesting very juicy quite racy in  parts but actually raises some very core  fundamental policy questions and i for  one certainly hope that  regulators and politicians on both sides  of the atlantic listen to very seriously  so thank you nice director julian  monday

